ok - just-admitted workers count as reserved memory, so a burst is admitted only while it fits
ok - a reservation stops counting after reserve_secs
ok - the gate closes at 85% and reopens only below 70%
ok - nearly exhausted swap closes the gate even with RAM to spare
ok - --override admits a captain-directed worker and still counts it
ok - without /proc/meminfo the gate admits with a warning instead of blocking
ok - config/memory-gate is validated, tunable, and can switch the gate off
ok - the critical line stops only the single largest heavy job in a task's tree and tells its worker
ok - a heavy job whose subtree contains a worker agent is never stopped
ok - a critical episode with nothing stoppable is reported once per episode
ok - one browser tree over its ceiling is stopped early, alone, even while total memory is fine
ok - one test run over the configured job ceiling is stopped and its worker told
ok - freed memory announces deferred work once, and admission clears it
ok - the dispatch queue puts the flagship project first, then keeps request order
ok - the watchdog loop is a singleton that runs only while the home has work
ok - fm-spawn defers a fresh spawn over the gate, admits it on --memory-override, and refuses the override for a secondmate
ok - a deferred relaunch is announced as one when room frees and dropped when its task is torn down
ok - a job is sized by summed PSS, falling back to RSS where PSS is unreadable
ok - a held gate lock postpones only the gate update, never the job stops
ok - trimming surfaced events keeps every later event for the next poll
# all fm-memory-watchdog tests passed
